# What a command gets, in QEMU (TASK-18 step 7, ADR-0153): its handle table
# and each entry's requests (INV-0153-1, INV-0153-2), link objects (rule 5,
# INV-0153-2), and the report of a refused path (the native half of
# INV-0153-7). The volume is rootfs/ with tests/shell/grants-root/ over
# it: /t/secret.txt, and in /t/w inside.txt, real/deep.txt and the links
# in-link -> inside.txt, out-link -> ../secret.txt, sub -> real, up -> ..
# `probe` lists its slots, tries every request through each entry and opens
# each PATH twice, to read and to change.
! panicked
! storage failed
< Type help to begin
< \r\n~> 
> cd /t/w\r
< \r\n/t/w> 
# INV-0153-1: nothing designated. Its job, its three streams, its own heap
# (it made that itself), the current directory's connection and the report
# channel; nothing else, and the directory reads and lists only.
> probe\r
< slots: 2:job 1001:stream-read 1002:stream-write 1003:stream-write 1004:memory 1005:channel 1006:channel
< entry /t/w directory: stat=ok widen=not granted list=ok create=not granted mkdir=not granted remove=not granted sync=not granted rename=not granted token=ok share=not granted narrow=not granted unnamed=not granted
< \r\n/t/w> 
# INV-0153-2: each kind gives its rights and no more (ADR-0153 rule 2).
> probe --read inside.txt --change real/deep.txt --tree real\r
# A path inside an entry that gives it as much takes no entry of its own
# (review S3, S9): the tree covers real/deep.txt, the current directory
# inside.txt. The first entry follows the slots directly.
< slots: 2:job 1001:stream-read 1002:stream-write 1003:stream-write 1004:memory 1005:channel 1006:channel 1007:channel 1008:channel
^ \r\nentry /t/w/real directory: stat=ok widen=not granted list=ok create=ok mkdir=ok remove=ok sync=ok rename=ok token=ok share=not granted narrow=not granted unnamed=not granted
< entry /t/w directory: stat=ok widen=not granted list=ok create=not granted mkdir=not granted remove=not granted sync=not granted rename=not granted token=ok share=not granted narrow=not granted unnamed=not granted
< \r\n/t/w> 
# A file to change, alone: an entry of its own, which reads and writes it
# and nothing more.
> probe --change real/deep.txt\r
< entry /t/w/real/deep.txt file: stat=ok widen=not granted read=ok write=ok sync=ok
< \r\n/t/w> 
# A name to create: its directory, narrowed to that one name.
> probe --create real/new.txt\r
< entry /t/w/real directory: stat=ok widen=not granted list=not granted create=not granted mkdir=not granted remove=not granted sync=ok
< \r\n/t/w> 
# INV-0153-7 (native half) and INV-0153-2 through a link: a path outside
# every entry is refused, twice, and so is one that a link inside an entry
# leads outside; both are one path, reported once. A missing path inside
# an entry is not found, and never reported.
> probe ../secret.txt inside.txt up/secret.txt missing.txt\r
< path ../secret.txt: read=not granted change=not granted\r\npath ../secret.txt: read=not granted change=not granted
< path inside.txt: read=ok change=not granted
< path up/secret.txt: read=not granted change=not granted\r\npath up/secret.txt: read=not granted change=not granted
< path missing.txt: read=not found change=not granted\r\npath missing.txt: read=not found change=not granted
<   hint: probe tried to read /t/secret.txt, which it was not given\r\n  to allow it: +r /t probe ../secret.txt
x tried to read /t/w/
< \r\n/t/w> 
# Rule 5: a designated link inside the current directory gives its target;
# one leading outside is refused with the link and its target, until a
# grant covers the target; grants shows the targets.
> cat in-link\r
< \r\ninside
< \r\n/t/w> 
> cat out-link\r
< zosh: /t/w/out-link: leads through a link to /t/secret.txt, outside the current directory and this line's grants\r\n  hint: to give it, put +r /t/secret.txt before the command
x \nsecret\r
< \r\n/t/w> 
> +r .. cat out-link\r
< \r\nsecret
< \r\n/t/w> 
> grants cat in-link sub/deep.txt\r
<   /t/w/inside.txt      read (operand FILE, through a link at /t/w/in-link)\r\n  /t/w/real/deep.txt   read (operand FILE, through a link at /t/w/sub/deep.txt)
< \r\n/t/w> 
> cat sub/deep.txt\r
< \r\ndeep
< \r\n/t/w> 
# A narrowed entry's directory through a link that leads outside.
> rm up/secret.txt\r
< zosh: /t/w/up: leads through a link to /t, outside the current directory and this line's grants\r\n  hint: to give it, put +w /t before the command
< \r\n/t/w> 
> cat ../secret.txt\r
< \r\nsecret
< \r\n/t/w> 
> poweroff\r
